WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:05.670
We need a numerical procedure,
which is easy in one direction

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.660
and hard in the other.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.960
This brings us to modular
arithmetic, also known

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.050
as clock arithmetic.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:17.250
For example, to
find 46 mod 12, we

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.040
could take a rope
of length 46 units

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.160
and wrap it around a
clock of 12 units, which

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:28.380
is called the modulus, and where
the rope ends is the solution.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.070
So we say 46 mod 12
is congruent to 10.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:34.110
Easy.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:39.760
Now, to make this work, we use
a prime modulus, such as 17.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.820
Then we find a
primitive root of 17.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.600
In this case, 3, which has this
important property that, when

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.380
raised to different
exponents, the solution

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.410
distributes uniformly
around the clock.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.430
3 is known as the generator.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:00.970
If we raise 3 to any
exponent x, then the solution

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:05.960
is equally likely to be any
integer between 0 and 17.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.040
Now, the reverse
procedure is hard.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.530
Say, given 12, find the exponent
3 needs to be raised to.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.240
This is called the
discrete logarithm problem.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.270
And now we have our one way
function, easy to perform,

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:24.080
but hard to reverse.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:27.810
Given 12, we would have to
resort to trial and error

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.040
to find matching exponents.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.710
How hard is this?

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.100
Well, with small
numbers it's easy,

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.420
but if we use a prime modulus,
which is hundreds of digits

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.060
long, it becomes
impractical to solve.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.475
Even if you had access to all
computational power on earth,

00:01:45.475 --> 00:01:47.880
it could take thousands
of years to run

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.690
through all possibilities.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:51.940
So the strength of
a one way function

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:56.100
is based on the time
needed to reverse it.

